Z||000005||Carrolles||08-07-2001||10:16 AM||||The mechanical parking brake mechanism is preventing you from pressing the pistons back into the cylinder bores.  Disconnect the parking brake lever on the back side of the caliper and place a large socket over the shaft.  Now use a c-clamp against the piston and the socket to press the piston back into the cylinder.  The parking brake shaft will turn while piston is being pushed back in.  Once done, re-attach the parking brake lever and adjust accordingly.<P>Some people also use the caliper piston tool to screw the piston into the bore.  I've never done it that way.<P>If you don't already have one you might want to at least get a Haynes or Chilton manual.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000006||starfighter007@msn.com||08-07-2001||08:51 PM||||Well when you have stuck bolts like that you should try useing small pair of vice grips .Ihave a feeling that you maybe better off at this point to get you some pre loaded calipers from Auto Zone and some new brake lines while your at it.If you have never done rear disc.brakes before they can be a little tricky.It will be cheaper and safer for you. I am not sure if you done any damage to your calipers while they are off trying to compress the piston into the sleeve better be safe then sorry .brakes may fail you when you need them most or God forbid the caliper hangs up and you continue driving and start a fire...<p>[This message has been edited by starfighter007@msn.com (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||4
